          %h2#idea Idea
          %p There are many flavours of category:
          %ul
            %li
              ordinary
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+categories"} enriched categories
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+categories"} internal categories
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibred+categories"} fibred categories
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/indexed+categories"} indexed categories
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+categories"} monoidal categories
          %p and so on. In each flavour of category theory, one finds essentially the same definitions and theorems:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaves"} presheaves
                and the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma"} Yoneda lemma
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunctions"} adjunctions
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor+theorems"} adjoint functor theorems
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads"} monads
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monadicity+theorems"} monadicity theorems
            %li
              %p
                theories of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/accessible+category"} accessible
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+categories"} locally presentable categories
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabriel--Ulmer+duality"} duality theorems
          %p and so on. Rather than define these concepts and prove these theorems in each of the different flavours of category theory independently (as has been done traditionally), one would rather be able to define these concepts and prove these theorems abstractly, and then specialise to each of these flavours, obtaining for instance the statements for enriched category theory as a corollary of the more abstract version. This is the motivation for formal category theory.
          %p
            %strong Formal category theory
            may be thought of as applying the philosophy of category theory to category theory itself. Typically, this takes the form of applying 2-dimensional category theories (e.g.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category theory
            or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+category"} double category theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+mathematics"} synthetic
            approach to category theory.
          %p
            This may be seen as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorification
            of the approach of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+set+theory"} structural set theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+mathematics"} synthetic
            approach to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            is intended as an axiomatisation of the logical nature of the archetypical topos
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            %a{:href => "Yoneda+structure#SW78"} Street &amp; Walters
            introduced
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+structures"} Yoneda structures
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-topos"} 2-topos
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
          %h2#approaches Approaches
          %p An important question is: what is the appropriate setting in which to study the formal theory of categories? There is not one obvious answer, and several approaches have been proposed in the literature.
          %p Roughly speaking, there are four main approaches (listed in chronological order of the first time they were proposed).
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-categories"} 2-categories
                with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/property-like+structure"} property-like structure
              %p
                (cf.
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#Gray74"} Gray 1974
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+structures"} Yoneda structures
              %p
                (cf.
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "Yoneda+structure#SW78"} Street &amp; Walters 1978
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proarrow+equipments"}> proarrow equipments
                \/
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/framed+bicategories"}> framed bicategories
                \/(augmented)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+equipments"} virtual equipments
              %p
                (cf.
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "2-category+equipped+with+proarrows#Wood82"} Wood 1982
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a{:href => "2-category+equipped+with+proarrows#Wood85"} 1985
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a{:href => "framed+bicategory#Shulman08"} Shulman 2008
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a{:href => "generalized+multicategory#CruttwellShulman10"} Cruttwell &amp; Shulman 2010
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#Koudenburg22"} Koudenburg 2022
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax-idempotent+2-monads"} lax-idempotent 2-monads
              %p
                (cf.
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "lax-idempotent+2-monad#BF99"} Bunge &amp; Funk 1999
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/toddtrimble/published/Epistemologies"} epistemologies
          %p The relationship between the approaches, for the most part, is not laid out clearly in the literature, but a basic intuition is that:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-categories"} 2-categories
                may be equipped with various
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/property-like+structure"} property-like structure
                possessed by the 2-category
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-limits"} 2-limits
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-colimits"} 2-colimits
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponentials"} exponentials
            %li
              %p
                Yoneda structures are based on formalising the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+presheaves"} presheaf construction
            %li
              %p
                Proarrow equipments and related structures are based on formalising
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributors"} distributors
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profunctors"} profunctors
            %li
              %p
                Lax-idempotent pseudomonads (also called
                = succeed ")" do
                  %strong KZ-doctrines
                = succeed "-construction." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+cocompletion"} free cocompletion
          %p
            The first, purely 2-categorical, approach is unsufficient to capture
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+categories"} enriched categories
            (though captures many aspects of ordinary and internal category theory), and is therefore not viable as a general approach to formal category theory.
          %p
            On the other hand, in nice settings, e.g. in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category+theory"} enriched category theory
            for a well-behaved
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+of+enrichment"} base of enrichment
            (eg. a
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B%C3%A9nabou+cosmos"} Bénabou cosmos
          %p
            For instance, one may not in general have a
            = succeed "-construction" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf+category"} presheaf category
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+cocompletion"} free cocompletion
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax-idempotent+pseudomonads"} lax-idempotent pseudomonads
            are not applicable in general. Without enough
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coequalisers"} coequalisers
            in the base,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributors"} distributors
            do not compose, and so proarrow equipments and fibrant double categories are not applicable. For instance, for a general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+equipments"} virtual equipments
            and
            %span.newWikiWord
              augmented virtual equipments
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/augmented+virtual+equipments"} ?
            capture the structure of
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+categories"} enriched categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaves"} presheaves
            as in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Koudenburg22"} Koudenburg 2022
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+structure"} Yoneda structure
          %h2#theorems_in_formal_category_theory Theorems in formal category theory
          %p Below, we list some fundamental theorems in category theory that have been formulated in formal category theory.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                General results about
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weighted+limits"} weighted limits
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weighted+colimits"} weighted colimits
                are developed in
                %a{:href => "Yoneda+structure#SW78"} Street &amp; Walters 1978
                (for
                = succeed ");" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+structures"} Yoneda structures
                %a{:href => "2-category+equipped+with+proarrows#Wood82"} Wood 1982
                (for
                = succeed ");" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proarrow+equipments"} proarrow equipments
                %a{:href => "#AM23b"} Arkor &amp; McDermott 2023a
                (for
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+equipments"} virtual equipments
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor+theorem"} Adjoint functor theorems
                are proven in Proposition 21 of
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a{:href => "Yoneda+structure#SW78"} Street &amp; Walters 1978
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a{:href => "2-category+equipped+with+proarrows#Wood82"} Wood 1982
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "#ALL23"} Arkor, Di Liberti, Loregian
            %li
              %p
                An analogue of the
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijective-on-objects+functor"} bijective-on-objects
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful+functor"} fully faithful
                factorisation system is established in
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "2-category+equipped+with+proarrows#Wood85"} Wood 1985
            %li
              %p
                General results about
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads"} monads
                are developed in
                %a{:href => "Yoneda+structure#SW78"} Street &amp; Walters 1978
                (for
                = succeed ");" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+structures"} Yoneda structures
                %a{:href => "2-category+equipped+with+proarrows#Wood85"} Wood 1985
                (for
                = succeed ");" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proarrow+equipments"} proarrow equipments
                %a{:href => "#AM23b"} Arkor &amp; McDermott 2023a
                in the additional generality of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+monads"} relative monads
                (for
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+equipments"} virtual equipments
            %li
              %p
                The theory of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/total+categories"} total categories
                is developed in
                %a{:href => "Yoneda+structure#SW78"} Street &amp; Walters 1978
                (for
                = succeed ");" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+structures"} Yoneda structures
                %a{:href => "#Koudenburg22"} Koudenburg 2022
                (for
                = succeed ")." do
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    augmented virtual equipments
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/augmented+virtual+equipments"} ?
            %li
              %p
                A general duality theorem subsuming
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabriel%E2%80%93Ulmer+duality"} Gabriel–Ulmer duality
                (and many others) is proven in
                %a{:href => "#DL18"} Di Liberti &amp; Loregian 2023
                (for
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax-idempotent+pseudomonads"} lax-idempotent pseudomonads
            %li
              %p
                General results about
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaves"} presheaves
                are developed in
                %a{:href => "#Koudenburg22"} Koudenburg 2022
                (for
                = succeed ")." do
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    augmented virtual equipments
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/augmented+virtual+equipments"} ?
            %li
              %p
                A (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+monad"}> relative
                )
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monadicity+theorem"} monadicity theorem
                is proven in
                %a{:href => "#AM23b"} Arkor &amp; McDermott 2023a
                (for
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+equipments"} virtual equipments
            %li
              %p
                A pullback theorem for
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads"} monads
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+monads"} relative monads
                is proven in
                %a{:href => "#AM24"} Arkor &amp; McDermott 2024
                (for
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+equipments"} virtual equipments
          %p
            For instance, one motivation for the development of this theory in the setting of (augmented)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+equipments"} virtual equipments
            is to develop
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category+theory"} enriched category theory
            without the assumptions taken, for instance, by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Max+Kelly"} Kelly
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "enriched+category#Kelly82"} Basic concepts of enriched category theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+double+category"} virtual double category
          %h2#intramural_and_extramural_formal_category_theory Intramural and extramural formal category theory
          %p
            There are two distinct aspects to the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} theory of categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphisms"} monomorphisms
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphisms"} epimorphisms
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
            = succeed "." do
              %strong intramural category theory
          %p
            The other aspect is concerned with the relation between different categories, constructions on categories (e.g.
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+categories"} product categories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+categories"} functor categories
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functors"} adjoint functors
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            (which would be the study of formal category theory). We shall refer to this aspect as
            = succeed "." do
              %strong extramural category theory
          %p
            In the same way, there are both intramural and extramural aspects to formal category theory.
            %strong Intramural formal category theory
            is what we have meant by “formal category theory” above, i.e. developing category theory inside some 2-dimensional structure (e.g. a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+structure"} Yoneda structure
            or
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proarrow+equipment"} proarrow equipment
            %strong extramural category theory
            is the theory of such 2-dimensional structures, e.g. the theory of Yoneda structures, or the theory of proarrow equipments. Note that, just as extramural category theory is distinct from formal category theory, so too is extramural formal category theory distinct from what might be called “formal 2-category theory”, “formal 2-dimensional category theory”, or “formal formal category theory”.
          %p
            Extramural formal category theory is motivated by formal category theory. For instance, an important construction in extramural formal category theory is the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bimodule"} monads and bimodules construction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proarrow+equipment"} proarrow equipment
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Mod
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod(X)
            from an existing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proarrow+equipment"} proarrow equipment
            (there are analogues for other approaches to formal category theory). This provides a family of new examples of formal category theories, in which one can perform intramural formal category theory. For instance, proarrow equipments of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Mod
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod(X)
            are very well behaved, which equips them with a rich intramural category theory (as studied in
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "2-category+equipped+with+proarrows#Wood85"} Wood 1985
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+objects"} Kleisli objects
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg%E2%80%93Moore+objects"} Eilenberg–Moore objects
          %h2#related_pages Related pages
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category+theory"} enriched category theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCC"} ETCC
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-topos"} 2-topos
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+%28infinity%2C1%29-category+theory"} formal (infinity,1)-category theory
          %h2#references References
          %p
            An axiomatization of (just) the (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/very+large+category"}> very large
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-category"} 1-category
            of all categories, but fully in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+logic"} formal logic
            is the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+theory+of+the+category+of+categories"} elementary theory of the category of categories
            (ETCC) due to:
          %ul
            %li#Law66
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Category of Categories as a Foundation for Mathematics
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Samuel+Eilenberg"} S. Eilenberg
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D.+K.+Harrison"} D. K. Harrison
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S.+MacLane"} S. MacLane
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/H.+R%C3%B6hrl"} H. Röhrl
              (eds.):
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Proceedings+of+the+Conference+on+Categorical+Algebra+-+La+Jolla+1965"} Proceedings of the Conference on Categorical Algebra - La Jolla 1965
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-99902-4"} doi:10.1007/978-3-642-99902-4
          %p
            The undertaking of laying these foundations instead for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            of all categories is famously associated with:
          %ul
            %li#Gray74
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Gray"}> John Gray
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Adjointness+for+2-Categories"}
                    Formal category theory: adjointness for
                    = succeed "-categories" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mn 2
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 391
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BFb0061280"} doi:10.1007/BFb0061280
          %p Review and further discussion, including on the relationship between the different approaches:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ivan+Di+Liberti"}> Ivan Di Liberti
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simon+Henry"} Simon Henry
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Lieberman"} Mike Lieberman
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fosco+Loregian"} Fosco Loregian
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Formal category theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://tetrapharmakon.github.io/stuff/course_muni-formal.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/DLHLL-FormalCategoryTheory.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ivan+Di+Liberti"}> Ivan Di Liberti
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fosco+Loregian"} Fosco Loregian
                %em On the unicity of formal category theories
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.01594"}> arXiv:1901.01594
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            The relation between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax-idempotent+pseudomonads"} lax-idempotent pseudomonads
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+structures"} Yoneda structures
            is due to:
          %ul#WalkerYS
            %li
              Charles Walker,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Yoneda Structures and KZ Doctrines
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.08693"} arxiv
          %p
            Discussion in
            = succeed "-theory:" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+category"} double category
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Shulman"}> Michael Shulman
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Framed bicategories and monoidal fibrations
                %strong 20
                18 (2008) 650–738 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0706.1286"}> arXiv:0706.1286
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#CruttwellShulman10
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Geoff+Cruttwell"} Geoff Cruttwell
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"} Mike Shulman
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A unified framework for generalized multicategories
